    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  To understand how government works, we must understand its fundamental concepts: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">power</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">authority</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">legitimacy</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">sovereignty</span>. These concepts explain WHY people obey government, HOW government commands compliance, and what makes government's actions binding.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Power</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Power</strong> is the ability to influence or control the behaviour of others, with or without their consent. It is the capacity to make others do what you want, even against their will.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Power can be <strong>physical</strong> (military/police force), <strong>economic</strong> (wealth), or <strong>political</strong> (authority)</li>\n  <li>Max Weber's definition: Power is the ability to exercise one's will even against resistance</li>\n  <li>Political power is the ability of government to influence or control political decisions and behaviour</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Authority</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Authority</strong> is the <em>legitimate</em> (rightful) exercise of power — power that is accepted as valid and proper by those subject to it. It is power + acceptance.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Max Weber identified three types of authority:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Basis</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Traditional authority</td><td>Custom, tradition, heredity</td><td>Traditional rulers (emirs, obas, chiefs)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Charismatic authority</td><td>Personal charm, qualities, inspiration</td><td>Nelson Mandela, Martin Luther King Jr.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Legal-rational authority</td><td>Written laws, rules, and constitutions</td><td>Elected presidents, appointed judges</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Legitimacy</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Legitimacy</strong> is the quality that makes people accept government authority as rightful and binding — not just because they are forced to, but because they believe it is proper. Sources of legitimacy:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Elections:</strong> Government chosen through free and fair elections (democratic legitimacy)</li>\n  <li><strong>Constitutional legality:</strong> Government operates within the constitution</li>\n  <li><strong>Traditional/religious legitimacy:</strong> Authority based on tradition or divine sanction</li>\n  <li><strong>Performance/delivery:</strong> Government gains legitimacy by delivering goods and services</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Sovereignty</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Sovereignty</strong> is the supreme, absolute, and final authority in a state — the highest power that is not subject to any higher authority. Jean Bodin (1576) first systematically defined sovereignty.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Internal sovereignty</td><td>Supreme authority over all persons and groups within the state's territory</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>External sovereignty</td><td>Independence from external control — the state is not subject to any other state</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Legal sovereignty</td><td>The body that legally makes the laws (parliament/constitution)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Political sovereignty</td><td>The body that really holds power in practice (e.g., the electorate in a democracy)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The KEY difference between power and authority: Power can be exercised without legitimacy (dictators have power but lack authority). Authority is LEGITIMATE power — it is accepted as right. Sovereignty is the SUPREME power — above all others within the state. In Nigeria, sovereignty belongs to the people (Section 14(2) of the 1999 Constitution).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Power = ability to control behaviour (with or without consent). Authority = legitimate power (accepted as right). Three types of authority (Weber): traditional, charismatic, legal-rational. Legitimacy = the quality making authority acceptable. Sovereignty = supreme, absolute, final power. Internal sovereignty = control over territory. External sovereignty = independence from other states.\n</div>\n  ",
