    "topic": "Basic Principles of Government",
    "topicCode": "SS1-GOV-03",
    "module": "Introduction to Government",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Modern democratic government is built on fundamental principles that protect citizens from arbitrary rule and ensure accountable governance. The three most important principles are: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Rule of Law</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Separation of Powers</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Checks and Balances</span>. Together, these principles ensure no single person or group can abuse power.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Rule of Law</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The Rule of Law, as formulated by A.V. Dicey (1885), means that the law is supreme — everyone, including the government and its officials, is subject to and equal before the law. Dicey's three pillars:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Supremacy of the law:</strong> No one is above the law; only the law rules, not arbitrary power</li>\n  <li><strong>Equality before the law:</strong> Everyone is subject to the same law, regardless of status or position</li>\n  <li><strong>Courts as the source of rights:</strong> Individual rights are secured through the ordinary courts, not government discretion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Separation of Powers</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Formulated by Baron de Montesquieu (The Spirit of the Laws, 1748), separation of powers means that the three functions of government should be placed in different hands to prevent tyranny:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Function</th><th>In Nigeria</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Legislature</td><td>Makes laws</td><td>National Assembly (Senate + House of Representatives)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Executive</td><td>Implements/enforces laws and policies</td><td>President, Vice-President, Federal Executive Council</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Judiciary</td><td>Interprets laws, resolves disputes, administers justice</td><td>Supreme Court, Court of Appeal, Federal High Court, etc.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Checks and Balances</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Checks and balances is the system whereby each arm of government has powers to limit and oversee the other arms, preventing any one arm from becoming too powerful:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Legislature checks Executive:</strong> Approves budget, confirms appointments, impeachment, legislative oversight</li>\n  <li><strong>Executive checks Legislature:</strong> President can veto bills (withhold assent)</li>\n  <li><strong>Judiciary checks Both:</strong> Can declare laws or executive actions unconstitutional (judicial review)</li>\n  <li><strong>Legislature checks Judiciary:</strong> Approves judicial appointments, can amend laws declared unconstitutional</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-svg-wrap\">\n  <svg class=\"learn-svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <rect width=\"480\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#0f0020\" rx=\"10\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Separation of Powers and Checks &amp; Balances</text>\n    <polygon points=\"240,30 310,80 170,80\" fill=\"#4B0082\" stroke=\"#D4AF37\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\">LEGISLATURE</text>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"7\">Makes Laws</text>\n    <rect x=\"30\" y=\"100\" width=\"140\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1a0030\" stroke=\"#28c840\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#28c840\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\">EXECUTIVE</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"7\">Implements Laws</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"7\">and Policies</text>\n    <rect x=\"310\" y=\"100\" width=\"140\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1a0030\" stroke=\"#c8b4f0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"380\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8b4f0\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\">JUDICIARY</text>\n    <text x=\"380\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"7\">Interprets Laws</text>\n    <text x=\"380\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"7\">Resolves Disputes</text>\n    <line x1=\"170\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#D4AF37\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"310\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#D4AF37\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"170\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#D4AF37\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">Each arm checks and balances the others — preventing tyranny</text>\n  </svg>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Montesquieu = Separation of Powers. A.V. Dicey = Rule of Law. Remember Dicey's THREE pillars: (1) Supremacy of law, (2) Equality before law, (3) Courts protect rights. In presidential systems (like Nigeria), separation of powers is more complete. In parliamentary systems (like UK), the executive comes from the legislature — less strict separation.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Rule of Law (Dicey): supremacy of law, equality before law, courts protect rights. Separation of Powers (Montesquieu): legislature makes laws, executive implements, judiciary interprets. Checks and Balances: each arm limits the others (veto, impeachment, judicial review). These three principles prevent tyranny and protect democratic governance.\n</div>\n  ",
