    "topic": "Types and Characteristics of Government",
    "topicCode": "SS1-GOV-04",
    "module": "Forms of Government",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Governments can be organised and structured in different ways depending on how power is distributed between levels of government. The three main types based on territorial distribution of power are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">unitary</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">federal</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">confederal</span> systems. Each has distinct features, advantages, and disadvantages.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Unitary System</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">In a <strong>unitary system</strong>, all powers are concentrated in the central government. Sub-national units exist but derive their authority from and are subordinate to the central government.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Power concentration</td><td>Central government is supreme; regions/states are subordinate</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Constitutional status of regions</td><td>Not guaranteed — central government can alter or abolish them</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Law supremacy</td><td>National law always supersedes local law</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>United Kingdom, France, China, Japan</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Merits:</strong> Strong central authority; uniform laws and policies; easier to coordinate national development; less expensive to run; faster decision-making.<br>\n<strong>Demerits:</strong> Can ignore regional differences; may become tyrannical; poor minority protection; over-centralisation stifles local initiative.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Federal System</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">In a <strong>federal system</strong>, powers are constitutionally divided between the central government and component state/regional governments. Both levels are supreme within their own assigned spheres.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Power distribution</td><td>Divided between federal and state governments by the constitution</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Constitutional protection</td><td>States guaranteed constitutional existence — central government cannot abolish them</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Written constitution</td><td>Essential (defines the division of powers)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Nigeria, USA, India, Australia, Canada, Germany</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Merits:</strong> Accommodates diversity; decentralises power; protects minorities; promotes local development and autonomy.<br>\n<strong>Demerits:</strong> Can be expensive; may weaken national unity; duplication of functions; conflicts between levels of government.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Confederal System</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">In a <strong>confederation</strong>, independent states voluntarily come together and create a weak central body to which they delegate limited powers. The member states retain ultimate sovereignty.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Central body has only the powers delegated to it by member states</li>\n  <li>Member states can withdraw at any time</li>\n  <li>Examples: USA under the Articles of Confederation (1781–1789); the Confederate States of America; the European Union (somewhat confederal); the West African Customs Union</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Merits:</strong> Preserves state sovereignty; flexible; easy to form.<br>\n<strong>Demerits:</strong> Weak central authority; no direct relationship with citizens; prone to disintegration.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the power hierarchy: Unitary = centre supreme; Federal = shared/divided; Confederal = states supreme. Nigeria is FEDERAL — it has a written constitution dividing powers between the federal government and 36 state governments. The UK is UNITARY — no written constitution divides powers between Westminster and regions.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Unitary: all power at centre (UK, France). Federal: power divided between centre and states by constitution (Nigeria, USA). Confederal: member states supreme, central body weak (rare). Federalism requires a written constitution, independent judiciary, and bicameral legislature. The best system depends on a country's size, diversity, and historical context.\n</div>\n  ",
