    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Beyond the territorial distribution of power, governments also differ in how the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">executive</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">legislature</span> relate to each other. The two main systems based on this relationship are the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">parliamentary system</span> (also called cabinet system) and the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">presidential system</span>. Nigeria practises the presidential system.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Parliamentary (Cabinet) System</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">In a parliamentary system, the executive is drawn from and remains accountable to the legislature. The government is led by a Prime Minister who is the leader of the majority party in parliament.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Executive source</td><td>Executive (Cabinet/PM) comes from the legislature</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Head of State vs Government</td><td>Separate: monarch or president (ceremonial) + Prime Minister (real executive)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Legislature's power over executive</td><td>Can remove executive through vote of no confidence</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Executive's power over legislature</td><td>PM can dissolve parliament and call elections</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>United Kingdom, India, Canada, Australia, Germany</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Merits:</strong> Quick decision-making; executive accountable to legislature; prevents deadlock between arms; reflects majority support.<br>\n<strong>Demerits:</strong> Government instability (frequent no-confidence votes); party dominance; weak separation of powers; potential for executive dominance of legislature.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Presidential System</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">In a presidential system, the executive and legislature are separately elected and are independent of each other. The President is both head of state and head of government.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Executive source</td><td>Directly elected by the people (separate from legislature)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Head of State and Government</td><td>Same person (the President)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fixed term</td><td>President serves fixed term; cannot be removed by legislature except by impeachment</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Separation of powers</td><td>Strict — executive, legislature, and judiciary are independent</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Nigeria, USA, Brazil, Mexico, Ghana</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Merits:</strong> Stable government (fixed term); clear separation of powers; president has direct mandate; executive not dependent on legislature.<br>\n<strong>Demerits:</strong> Potential deadlock between president and legislature; president may become authoritarian; expensive to run; rigid (difficult to remove bad president before term ends).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Comparison Table</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Parliamentary</th><th>Presidential</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Executive source</td><td>From legislature</td><td>Directly elected</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Head of State/Govt</td><td>Separate (Monarch/PM)</td><td>Same person (President)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Removal of executive</td><td>Vote of no confidence</td><td>Impeachment only</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Separation of powers</td><td>Weak (fusion)</td><td>Strict</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Government stability</td><td>Less stable</td><td>More stable (fixed term)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Nigeria ADOPTED the presidential system in 1979 (Second Republic) and has maintained it in the 1999 Constitution. Before that, Nigeria used the parliamentary system (1960–1966). The KEY difference: parliamentary = executive from legislature (vote of no confidence can remove PM). Presidential = executive directly elected (only impeachment removes president).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Parliamentary: executive from legislature; PM leads cabinet; vote of no confidence removes government (UK, India). Presidential: president directly elected; serves fixed term; strict separation of powers; removed only by impeachment (Nigeria, USA). Nigeria practises presidential system since 1979/1999.\n</div>\n  ",
