    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">political ideology</span> is a coherent set of beliefs, values, and ideas about how society should be organised and governed — particularly regarding ownership of property, distribution of wealth, individual freedom, and the role of the state. The major ideologies are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">capitalism</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">socialism</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">communism</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">fascism</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Capitalism</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Capitalism is an economic and political ideology based on private ownership of the means of production and free market principles. Key features:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Private ownership of property and means of production</li>\n  <li>Free market and competition (supply and demand determine prices)</li>\n  <li>Profit motive — businesses operate to earn profit</li>\n  <li>Minimal government interference in the economy (laissez-faire)</li>\n  <li>Individual freedom and rights are paramount</li>\n  <li>Examples: USA, UK, Western Europe</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Socialism</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Socialism advocates collective or state ownership of key means of production for equitable distribution of wealth. Features:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>State or collective ownership of major industries (not all property)</li>\n  <li>Planned or mixed economy (some state control)</li>\n  <li>Emphasis on equality and social welfare (education, health for all)</li>\n  <li>Government actively redistributes wealth through taxation and welfare</li>\n  <li>Examples: Sweden (social democracy), Cuba (elements), Tanzania under Nyerere</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Communism</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Communism (Marx and Engels, The Communist Manifesto, 1848) is a radical form of socialism that advocates the abolition of private property and the creation of a classless, stateless society:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>No private ownership of property — all property owned collectively/by the state</li>\n  <li>State controls all means of production and distribution</li>\n  <li>Classless society — no bourgeoisie (capitalist class) vs proletariat (working class)</li>\n  <li>Goal: 'From each according to his ability, to each according to his needs'</li>\n  <li>Single party (vanguard party of the proletariat) usually governs</li>\n  <li>Examples: Former USSR, China, Cuba, North Korea</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Fascism</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Fascism is an extreme right-wing, authoritarian, ultranationalist ideology that glorifies the state and nation above individuals. Features:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Authoritarian single-party rule (no opposition)</li>\n  <li>Extreme nationalism — the nation/race is supreme</li>\n  <li>Strong leader (cult of the leader) — e.g., Mussolini, Hitler</li>\n  <li>Militarism — aggressive use of military power</li>\n  <li>Suppression of opposition and minority rights</li>\n  <li>Examples: Italy under Mussolini (1922–1943), Germany under Hitler (Nazism, 1933–1945)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Ideology</th><th>Property</th><th>Economy</th><th>Government type</th><th>Individual rights</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Capitalism</td><td>Private</td><td>Free market</td><td>Democracy (usually)</td><td>Paramount</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Socialism</td><td>Mixed</td><td>Mixed/planned</td><td>Democracy or authoritarian</td><td>Balanced with equality</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Communism</td><td>State/collective</td><td>Centrally planned</td><td>Single party authoritarian</td><td>Subordinate to state</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fascism</td><td>Private (state-directed)</td><td>State-controlled capitalism</td><td>Authoritarian single party</td><td>Suppressed</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key distinctions: Capitalism = private ownership + free market. Socialism = mixed/state ownership + welfare emphasis. Communism = NO private property + classless society + single party (extreme socialism). Fascism = extreme nationalism + authoritarian + glorifies the state (opposite end from communism on individual rights, but both authoritarian).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Capitalism: private ownership, free market, profit motive, individual freedom (USA, UK). Socialism: collective ownership of key industries, welfare state, equality (Sweden). Communism: no private property, classless society, single party, state controls all (former USSR, China). Fascism: extreme nationalism, authoritarian, militarism, suppresses rights (Mussolini's Italy, Hitler's Germany).\n</div>\n  ",
