    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">constitution</span> is the fundamental law of a state — a body of basic rules and principles by which a state is governed. It defines the structure of government, distributes powers among its organs, protects citizens' rights, and sets the limits of governmental authority. The constitution is the supreme law; all other laws must conform to it.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Constitution</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A constitution can be defined as:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The totality of rules, principles, and conventions by which a state is governed</li>\n  <li>The fundamental law that defines the organisation and powers of government and the rights of citizens</li>\n  <li>In Nigeria: the 1999 Constitution (as amended) is the supreme law (Section 1)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Sources of a Constitution</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Historical documents:</strong> Magna Carta (1215), Bill of Rights (1689)</li>\n  <li><strong>Judicial decisions (case law):</strong> Court rulings that establish constitutional principles</li>\n  <li><strong>Conventions:</strong> Established practices and customs not legally binding but observed (e.g., UK Cabinet government)</li>\n  <li><strong>Statutes (Acts of Parliament):</strong> Acts of the legislature with constitutional significance</li>\n  <li><strong>Constitutional conferences:</strong> Deliberate drafting by representatives (e.g., Nigeria's 1999 Constitution)</li>\n  <li><strong>Doctrines of learned writers:</strong> Works of constitutional scholars</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types of Constitution</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Classification</th><th>Types</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>By form</td><td>Written (codified) vs Unwritten (uncodified)</td><td>Written: Nigeria, USA; Unwritten: UK, New Zealand</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>By amendment procedure</td><td>Rigid vs Flexible</td><td>Rigid: USA, Nigeria; Flexible: UK</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>By distribution of power</td><td>Federal vs Unitary</td><td>Federal: Nigeria, USA; Unitary: UK, France</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Features of a Good Constitution</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Supremacy — constitution is supreme over all other laws</li>\n  <li>Clarity — provisions must be clear and unambiguous</li>\n  <li>Flexibility — can be amended to meet changing circumstances</li>\n  <li>Bill of Rights — fundamental rights of citizens are guaranteed</li>\n  <li>Separation of powers — functions of government are clearly distributed</li>\n  <li>Independence of judiciary — courts are insulated from executive pressure</li>\n  <li>Popular consent — constitution must reflect the will of the people</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Importance of a Constitution</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Defines the structure, powers, and limits of government</li>\n  <li>Protects fundamental rights of citizens</li>\n  <li>Prevents arbitrary exercise of power</li>\n  <li>Promotes political stability and continuity</li>\n  <li>Guides the electoral process and democratic transitions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Rigid constitution = difficult to amend (requires special procedures, e.g., two-thirds majority + state assemblies in Nigeria). Flexible = easily amended like ordinary laws (e.g., UK). Written = in one document. Unwritten = not in a single document but scattered in statutes, conventions, and court decisions (UK's constitution is unwritten but not non-existent).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Constitution = fundamental law of the state. Sources: historical documents, case law, conventions, statutes, constitutional conferences. Types: written/unwritten; rigid/flexible; federal/unitary. Nigeria has a written, rigid, federal constitution (1999). UK has an unwritten, flexible, unitary constitution. A good constitution is supreme, clear, flexible, protects rights, and separates powers.\n</div>\n  ",
