    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Rule of Law</span> is one of the most important principles of democratic governance. Formulated by A.V. Dicey in 1885, it means that the law — not the arbitrary will of rulers — governs everyone. While the rule of law is an ideal, its application in practice faces several <span class=\"learn-keyword\">limitations</span>, especially in developing democracies.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Rule of Law (A.V. Dicey's Formulation)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Dicey identified three pillars:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>1. Supremacy of the law:</strong> No one is above the law; government must act under legal authority — not arbitrary power or discretion</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Equality before the law:</strong> All persons — rich or poor, ruler or citizen — are equal before the law and subject to the same courts</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Courts as the source of rights:</strong> The rights of individuals are determined and protected by the ordinary courts, not by government decree</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Importance of Rule of Law</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Prevents arbitrary rule and dictatorship</li>\n  <li>Protects individual rights and freedoms</li>\n  <li>Promotes justice and equal treatment</li>\n  <li>Creates a stable, predictable legal environment for business and development</li>\n  <li>Enhances government accountability</li>\n  <li>Builds citizens' confidence in government and courts</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Limitations of Rule of Law</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Limitation</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>State of emergency</td><td>Emergency laws temporarily suspend some rights (e.g., during war, crisis)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Immunity clauses</td><td>Some officials (e.g., presidents, legislators) enjoy immunity from prosecution while in office</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Delegated legislation</td><td>Wide executive discretion through statutory instruments may bypass scrutiny</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Retroactive legislation</td><td>Laws made to apply to past acts violate the principle (ex post facto laws)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Poverty and inequality</td><td>The poor cannot always afford legal representation — equality is theoretical, not practical</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Judicial corruption/bias</td><td>If judges are corrupt or politically influenced, the rule of law fails</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Executive impunity</td><td>Powerful individuals/government officials escape prosecution due to political connections</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Delay in justice</td><td>'Justice delayed is justice denied' — slow courts undermine rule of law</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Rule of Law in Nigeria</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Nigeria's 1999 Constitution enshrines many Rule of Law principles</li>\n  <li>Challenges: executive impunity, corruption, delayed justice, weak enforcement institutions</li>\n  <li>Presidential immunity: Section 308 of the 1999 Constitution grants the President, Vice-President, Governors, and Deputy Governors immunity from civil and criminal proceedings while in office</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The most tested aspect of Rule of Law is Dicey's THREE pillars (supremacy of law, equality before law, courts protect rights) AND the limitations. In Nigeria, Section 308 (immunity of executive office holders) is a key limitation — presidents and governors cannot be sued while in office.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Rule of Law (Dicey): no one above the law; all equal before law; courts protect rights. Importance: prevents dictatorship, protects rights, ensures justice. Limitations: emergency powers, immunity clauses, poverty limiting access to justice, judicial corruption, retroactive laws, executive impunity, slow courts. Nigeria's Section 308 = immunity for executive office holders = key limitation.\n</div>\n  ",
