    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Separation of powers</span> is the constitutional principle that the three functions of government — legislative, executive, and judicial — should be exercised by separate and independent organs. This prevents any single body from accumulating all governmental power and becoming tyrannical. Its application differs between <span class=\"learn-keyword\">presidential</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">parliamentary</span> systems.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Origin</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Baron de Montesquieu (1748) argued in <em>The Spirit of the Laws</em> that liberty is impossible where the same person or body holds legislative AND executive OR judicial power. The three functions must be separated:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Legislative power:</strong> Makes laws for the state</li>\n  <li><strong>Executive power:</strong> Implements and enforces laws; directs government policy</li>\n  <li><strong>Judicial power:</strong> Interprets laws; adjudicates disputes; punishes offenders</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Application in the Presidential System (Nigeria/USA)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Separation is <strong>strict</strong> — each arm is independently elected or appointed and operates independently</li>\n  <li>No individual can simultaneously hold positions in two arms (e.g., a senator cannot also be a minister in Nigeria)</li>\n  <li>Each arm has constitutional powers to check the others (checks and balances)</li>\n  <li>Nigeria: President cannot sit in NASS; NASS members cannot hold executive office; judges are appointed separately</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Application in the Parliamentary System (UK)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Separation is <strong>less strict</strong> — executive (Cabinet/PM) is drawn from the legislature (Parliament)</li>\n  <li>The executive sits in and is accountable to parliament — a fusion rather than separation</li>\n  <li>The judiciary is separate (House of Lords used to be the final court — replaced by independent Supreme Court in 2009)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Merits of Separation of Powers</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Prevents tyranny and concentration of power</li>\n  <li>Promotes accountability — each arm is accountable to others and citizens</li>\n  <li>Protects individual rights — courts can independently protect against executive/legislative overreach</li>\n  <li>Promotes efficiency — each arm focuses on its specific function</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Demerits of Separation of Powers</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Can cause deadlock between arms (especially presidential systems with divided government)</li>\n  <li>May slow down governance — arms must consult and cooperate</li>\n  <li>Arms may still overstep boundaries despite formal separation</li>\n  <li>Expensive — three separate institutions with their own structures</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Separation of powers is STRICTER in presidential systems (Nigeria, USA) than in parliamentary ones (UK, India). In Nigeria: a minister CANNOT be a member of NASS — they must resign their seat before taking executive office. In the UK, ministers ARE members of Parliament (executive comes from legislature).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Separation of powers (Montesquieu): legislature makes laws, executive implements, judiciary adjudicates. Presidential = strict separation (Nigeria, USA). Parliamentary = fusion (UK: executive from legislature). Merits: prevents tyranny, promotes accountability, protects rights. Demerits: deadlock, slower governance, costly.\n</div>\n  ",
