    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Delegated legislation</span> (also called subordinate or secondary legislation) refers to laws made by bodies other than parliament or the legislature, using authority that parliament has expressly granted. It allows the executive and other bodies to make detailed regulations without going through the full legislative process.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Delegated Legislation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Parliament (or the legislature) passes a <strong>Parent Act</strong> (enabling statute) that grants a minister, local authority, or other body the power to make detailed rules within the framework of the parent act. These subsidiary rules are delegated legislation.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Examples in Nigeria:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) makes banking regulations under the CBN Act</li>\n  <li>The Nigerian Communications Commission (NCC) makes telecom regulations under the NCC Act</li>\n  <li>The National Universities Commission (NUC) sets academic standards under its enabling act</li>\n  <li>Local Government bylaws on markets, sanitation, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Reasons for Delegated Legislation</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Lack of parliamentary time:</strong> Parliament cannot deal with all technical details</li>\n  <li><strong>Technical expertise:</strong> Regulatory bodies have specialist knowledge parliament lacks</li>\n  <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Regulations can be quickly updated without going through full parliamentary process</li>\n  <li><strong>Emergency situations:</strong> Rapid response needed (e.g., public health emergencies)</li>\n  <li><strong>Local needs:</strong> Local authorities can tailor rules to local circumstances</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types of Delegated Legislation</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Made by</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Statutory instruments/Orders-in-Council</td><td>Ministers/Executive</td><td>CBN monetary policy circulars</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bylaws</td><td>Local authorities, public corporations</td><td>LGA market bylaws</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Professional rules</td><td>Professional bodies</td><td>NBA rules, NMA regulations</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Merits and Demerits</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Merits</th><th>Demerits</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Saves parliamentary time</td><td>May undermine parliamentary supremacy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Uses specialist expertise</td><td>Democratic deficit — no public debate</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Flexible and quickly updated</td><td>May be used to expand executive power</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deals with technical detail parliament cannot handle</td><td>Volume and complexity make scrutiny difficult</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Enables rapid emergency response</td><td>Citizens may not be aware of all regulations</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember — delegated legislation is not made by parliament itself, but by OTHER BODIES authorised BY parliament through a parent/enabling act. It is still law (legally binding) but is subordinate to the parent act and the constitution. Courts can strike down delegated legislation that exceeds the authority granted (ultra vires).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Delegated legislation = laws made by bodies authorised by parliament through enabling acts. Reasons: save parliamentary time, need expertise, flexibility, emergencies. Types: statutory instruments (ministers), bylaws (local authorities), professional rules. Merits: efficient, expert, flexible. Demerits: democratic deficit, may expand executive power, hard to scrutinise. Courts can strike it down as ultra vires.\n</div>\n  ",
