    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Citizenship</span> is the legal status that confers membership of a state and entitles the holder to certain rights and protections while imposing corresponding duties. Citizens have a special relationship with their state that non-citizens (aliens/foreigners) do not fully share.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Citizenship</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Citizenship is the legal bond between a person and a state, conferring on the citizen: the protection of the state, the right to participate in government, and subjecting them to the state's laws and obligations.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Ways of Acquiring Nigerian Citizenship</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Sections 25–32 of the 1999 Constitution provide four ways:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>By birth</td><td>Born in Nigeria to a Nigerian father OR born anywhere to a Nigerian father (jus soli/sanguinis)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>By registration</td><td>Spouse (wife) of a Nigerian citizen can be registered as a citizen; persons of Nigerian descent</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>By naturalisation</td><td>Foreign-born person who meets requirements (15 years residence, good character, etc.) applies to the President</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>By honorary citizenship</td><td>President may confer citizenship on persons who have rendered distinguished services to Nigeria</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Rights of Nigerian Citizens</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Right to vote and be voted for in elections</li>\n  <li>Right to obtain a Nigerian passport and travel freely</li>\n  <li>Right to live and work anywhere in Nigeria</li>\n  <li>Right to state protection abroad (diplomatic protection)</li>\n  <li>Fundamental rights under Chapter IV (right to life, dignity, fair hearing, freedom of expression, etc.)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Duties and Obligations of Citizens</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Obey the laws of the state</li>\n  <li>Pay taxes and other lawful levies</li>\n  <li>Defend the state when called upon</li>\n  <li>Participate in the civic life of the nation (voting, jury service, etc.)</li>\n  <li>Respect the rights and freedoms of others</li>\n  <li>Protect public property and the environment</li>\n  <li>Declare true facts in census and other official surveys</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Four ways to acquire Nigerian citizenship: birth, registration, naturalisation, honorary. The most important distinction: BIRTH (automatic from birth to Nigerian father) vs NATURALISATION (15 years' residence, President's discretion, good character, etc.). Only citizens by birth can become President or Governor in Nigeria.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Citizenship = legal bond between person and state. Nigerian citizenship acquired by: birth, registration, naturalisation, honorary. Rights: vote, passport, work anywhere, diplomatic protection, fundamental rights. Duties: obey laws, pay taxes, defend state, participate civically. Only natural-born citizens can be President or Governor in Nigeria.\n</div>\n  ",
