    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Political socialisation</span> is the process by which individuals learn political values, attitudes, beliefs, and behaviours — developing their political identity and orientation. It explains how people come to hold political views and why different generations and societies have different political cultures.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Political Socialisation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Political socialisation is the lifelong process through which individuals acquire political knowledge, values, and attitudes that shape their political behaviour. It is how political culture is transmitted from one generation to the next.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Agents of Political Socialisation</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>How it socialises</th><th>Importance</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Family</td><td>First and most powerful agent — children absorb parents' political views, values, party affiliations</td><td>Most influential early socialisation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>School/Education</td><td>Civic education, national history, patriotism, democratic values; structured knowledge</td><td>Formal political education</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Peer groups</td><td>Friends, age-mates influence political attitudes, especially in adolescence</td><td>Reinforces or challenges family values</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mass media</td><td>TV, radio, newspapers, internet, social media shape political opinions and knowledge</td><td>Reaches mass audiences; increasingly dominant</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Religious institutions</td><td>Moral and ethical values that shape views on social and political issues</td><td>Strong in religious societies like Nigeria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Government/State</td><td>National symbols, ceremonies, propaganda, official national narratives</td><td>Official promotion of civic values and national identity</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Political parties</td><td>Party membership, campaigns, manifestos shape political identity and affiliation</td><td>Direct political education and mobilisation</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Importance of Political Socialisation</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Transmits political culture from generation to generation — maintains social cohesion</li>\n  <li>Produces informed, engaged citizens who can participate in democracy</li>\n  <li>Shapes political stability — common values reduce conflict</li>\n  <li>Determines voting behaviour and political participation patterns</li>\n  <li>Can promote or undermine democratic values depending on content</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The FAMILY is the MOST IMPORTANT primary agent of political socialisation — it is the first and most formative. School provides the most FORMAL political education. Mass media is the most WIDESPREAD in modern times. Political socialisation is a LIFELONG process — not just in childhood.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Political socialisation = process of acquiring political values, attitudes, and identity. Major agents: family (most influential), school (formal), peer groups, mass media (most widespread), religion, government, political parties. Importance: transmits political culture, produces engaged citizens, maintains stability. It is a lifelong process.\n</div>\n  ",
