    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Public opinion</span> refers to the collection of views and attitudes held by the general public on matters of political and social concern. It is a powerful force in democratic politics — governments are sensitive to public opinion because it shapes electoral outcomes and political legitimacy.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Public Opinion</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Public opinion is the aggregate of individual views, beliefs, and attitudes on political, social, and economic issues held by the general public. It is not unanimous — different groups hold different opinions. Key features:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Dynamic — changes with events, information, and circumstances</li>\n  <li>Diverse — different groups (class, region, religion) hold different views</li>\n  <li>Measurable — through polls, surveys, and elections</li>\n  <li>Influential — especially in democracies where governments need popular support</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Formation of Public Opinion</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Public opinion is formed through:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Mass media:</strong> TV, radio, newspapers, social media set the agenda and frame issues</li>\n  <li><strong>Political parties:</strong> Frame issues, present policy alternatives</li>\n  <li><strong>Interest groups and pressure groups:</strong> Lobby and campaign on specific issues</li>\n  <li><strong>Education:</strong> Shapes knowledge and critical thinking</li>\n  <li><strong>Personal experience:</strong> Direct experiences with government, economy, security</li>\n  <li><strong>Opinion leaders:</strong> Influential individuals (religious leaders, academics, celebrities) who shape views</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Measurement of Public Opinion</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Opinion polls/surveys:</strong> Structured questionnaires sampling a cross-section of the population</li>\n  <li><strong>Elections:</strong> The ultimate expression of public opinion on political leadership</li>\n  <li><strong>Referendums:</strong> Direct public votes on specific policy questions</li>\n  <li><strong>Protests and demonstrations:</strong> Visible expressions of public sentiment</li>\n  <li><strong>Media commentary:</strong> Letters to editors, phone-ins, social media trending topics</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Importance of Public Opinion in a Democracy</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Guides government policy — responsive governments react to public opinion</li>\n  <li>Holds leaders accountable — negative opinion can remove governments at elections</li>\n  <li>Reflects the public's values and preferences — guides legislative priorities</li>\n  <li>Can mobilise citizens for collective action (protest, election campaigns)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Limitations of Public Opinion</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Can be manipulated by propaganda and media bias</li>\n  <li>May be uninformed or based on misinformation</li>\n  <li>Volatile — can change rapidly with events</li>\n  <li>Minority views may be ignored (tyranny of the majority)</li>\n  <li>Difficult to measure accurately in large, diverse, illiterate populations</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Public opinion is NOT the same as unanimous opinion — it represents the AGGREGATE of diverse views. The most scientifically accurate measure is OPINION POLLS (surveys). Elections are the most POWERFUL expression of public opinion. Mass media is the MOST INFLUENTIAL agent in forming modern public opinion.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Public opinion = aggregate public views on political issues. Formed by: media, parties, interest groups, education, personal experience, opinion leaders. Measured by: polls, elections, referendums, protests, media. Important in democracy: guides policy, accountability. Limitations: manipulation, misinformation, volatility, difficult to measure accurately.\n</div>\n  ",
