    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">political party</span> is an organised group of people with shared political views and objectives who come together to contest elections, win political power, and implement their policies in government. Political parties are the primary vehicles of democratic competition and representation.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Definition of a Political Party</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A political party can be defined as an organised group of citizens with common political opinions and interests who seek to gain and exercise political power through elections and democratic processes.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Political Parties</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Characteristic</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Mass party</td><td>Large membership, broad-based; seeks mass support across classes</td><td>Historically, NPN, PDP (Nigeria)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Elite/cadre party</td><td>Small, select membership; focused on upper-class leadership</td><td>Conservative Party (UK, historically)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ideological party</td><td>Organised around a specific ideology (Marxist, religious, nationalist)</td><td>Communist Party of China</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Catch-all party</td><td>Seeks broadest possible support by moderating ideology — appeals to all voters</td><td>APC, PDP in contemporary Nigeria</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Functions of Political Parties</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Contesting elections:</strong> Primary function — fielding candidates to compete for office</li>\n  <li><strong>Political recruitment:</strong> Identifying, training, and presenting political leaders and candidates</li>\n  <li><strong>Policy formulation:</strong> Developing manifestos and policy platforms for governance</li>\n  <li><strong>Political education:</strong> Informing and educating citizens about political issues</li>\n  <li><strong>Political socialisation:</strong> Transmitting political values and building political identity</li>\n  <li><strong>Aggregation of interests:</strong> Bringing together diverse groups under a common platform</li>\n  <li><strong>Forming government:</strong> Winning party forms government and implements policies</li>\n  <li><strong>Opposition and scrutiny:</strong> Opposition parties check the government</li>\n  <li><strong>Legitimisation of the system:</strong> Parties operating within democratic rules legitimise the political system</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Importance of Political Parties</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Essential for democracy — competitive elections require organised parties</li>\n  <li>Provide voters with clear choices between policy alternatives</li>\n  <li>Train and recruit political leaders for governance</li>\n  <li>Organise the legislature (majority/opposition structure)\n  <li>Link citizens to government — channel of political participation</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The PRIMARY function of a political party (unlike a pressure group) is to CONTEST ELECTIONS and WIN POWER. Pressure groups aim to influence those in power but do NOT stand for election. In Nigeria, the current major parties are APC (ruling) and PDP (main opposition), with others like Labour Party gaining prominence.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Political party = organised group seeking power through elections. Types: mass, elite, ideological, catch-all. Functions: contest elections, recruit leaders, formulate policy, educate citizens, aggregate interests, form government, oppose. Importance: essential for democracy, provide policy choices, train leaders, organise legislature. Key distinction from pressure groups: parties contest elections; pressure groups do not.\n</div>\n  ",
