    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">party system</span> refers to the arrangement and number of political parties that effectively compete for power in a state. The three main types are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">one-party</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">two-party</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">multi-party</span> systems. Each has distinct features, advantages, and disadvantages.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. One-Party System</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Only one party is legally permitted to govern; no opposition parties allowed. The single party controls all aspects of political life.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>No genuine electoral competition</li>\n  <li>Party = state in most cases</li>\n  <li>Common in authoritarian and communist states</li>\n  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> China (CCP), North Korea (Workers' Party), Cuba (Communist Party), former USSR</li>\n  <li><strong>Merits:</strong> Political stability, rapid policy implementation, national unity, no destructive competition</li>\n  <li><strong>Demerits:</strong> No democracy, suppression of rights, abuse of power, corruption without accountability, no alternative</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Two-Party System</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Two major parties alternate in power; smaller parties may exist but rarely win government.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Clear voter choice between two alternatives</li>\n  <li>Usually produces stable, single-party governments</li>\n  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> USA (Democrats/Republicans), UK (Conservative/Labour)</li>\n  <li><strong>Merits:</strong> Stable government; clear alternation; avoids coalition instability; clear accountability</li>\n  <li><strong>Demerits:</strong> Limits voter choice; excludes third parties; may not represent all interests; both parties may converge to the centre (little real choice)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Multi-Party System</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Three or more parties compete effectively for power; government is usually through coalition.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Wide range of ideological options for voters</li>\n  <li>Often requires coalition governments</li>\n  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> Nigeria (APC, PDP, Labour Party, etc.), Germany, France, India</li>\n  <li><strong>Merits:</strong> More representative; diverse choices; minority groups represented; avoids dominance of one ideology</li>\n  <li><strong>Demerits:</strong> Coalition instability; difficulty forming stable government; fragmentation; voters may be confused by many parties</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Countries</th><th>Key Merit</th><th>Key Demerit</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>One-party</td><td>China, Cuba, N. Korea</td><td>Stability</td><td>No democracy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Two-party</td><td>USA, UK</td><td>Stable government</td><td>Limits choice</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Multi-party</td><td>Nigeria, Germany, France</td><td>Representation</td><td>Coalition instability</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Nigeria has a MULTI-PARTY system — many parties legally exist and contest elections. The USA has a DE FACTO two-party system (Democrats and Republicans dominate despite many registered parties). One-party systems are associated with authoritarian/communist regimes (China, Cuba). Two-party tends to produce stable single-party governments; multi-party tends to produce coalitions.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> One-party: single party rules, no competition (China, Cuba) — stable but authoritarian. Two-party: two main parties alternate (USA, UK) — stable government, limited choice. Multi-party: many parties compete (Nigeria, Germany) — representative but often coalition governments. Nigeria = multi-party system.\n</div>\n  ",
