    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Pressure groups</span> (also called interest groups) are organised associations that seek to influence government policy and public opinion on specific issues without seeking to hold political office themselves. They differ fundamentally from political parties in that they aim to INFLUENCE power, not WIN it.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Definition</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A pressure group is an organised group that uses various methods to influence government policy in favour of its members' interests or its cause, without seeking direct political power through elections.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Pressure Groups</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Definition</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Sectional/Protective groups</td><td>Protect the interests of a particular section of society (members' interests)</td><td>NMA (Nigerian Medical Association), NBA (Nigerian Bar Association), NLC (Nigeria Labour Congress), trade unions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Promotional/Cause groups</td><td>Promote a cause or idea in the public interest (not just members)</td><td>Amnesty International, FIDA, Civil Liberties Organisation, environmental groups</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Methods Used by Pressure Groups</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Lobbying:</strong> Directly contacting and persuading legislators, ministers, and officials</li>\n  <li><strong>Strikes and industrial action:</strong> Workers withhold labour to pressure government/employers (NLC, ASUU)</li>\n  <li><strong>Demonstrations and protests:</strong> Public marches and rallies to show breadth of concern</li>\n  <li><strong>Media campaigns:</strong> Using newspapers, TV, social media to shape public opinion</li>\n  <li><strong>Litigation (court action):</strong> Taking cases to court to force policy change</li>\n  <li><strong>Petitions:</strong> Gathering signatures to present formal demands</li>\n  <li><strong>Civil disobedience:</strong> Peaceful non-compliance with unjust laws or policies</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Differences between Pressure Groups and Political Parties</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Pressure Group</th><th>Political Party</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Primary aim</td><td>Influence policy</td><td>Win political power</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Elections</td><td>Do NOT contest elections</td><td>Contest elections</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Membership</td><td>Usually specific (professionals, workers, cause)</td><td>Broad — any citizen can join</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Focus</td><td>Usually single issue or narrow range</td><td>Wide range of policy issues</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Merits and Demerits of Pressure Groups</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Merits:</strong> Protect citizens' interests; supplement democracy; give voice to minorities; educate public; check government power</li>\n  <li><strong>Demerits:</strong> May represent narrow interests at expense of public good; well-funded groups may have disproportionate influence; can paralyse government (ASUU strikes disrupting education); may use undemocratic methods</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> KEY DISTINCTION: Pressure groups INFLUENCE power; parties SEEK power. NMA, NBA, NLC, ASUU are all SECTIONAL groups (protecting members' professional/occupational interests). Amnesty International, CLO are PROMOTIONAL (cause-based, public interest). ASUU strikes are a classic example of pressure group methods in Nigeria.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Pressure groups = organised groups influencing policy without seeking power. Types: sectional (NMA, NBA, NLC — protect members' interests) and promotional (Amnesty, CLO — advance a cause). Methods: lobbying, strikes, protests, media, litigation, petitions. Key difference from parties: parties contest elections, pressure groups do not.\n</div>\n  ",
