    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Public administration</span> is the implementation of government policies and the management of public affairs. While politicians make policy decisions, it is the public administration machinery — the civil service, public corporations, and regulatory agencies — that translates policy into actual programmes and services for citizens.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Public Administration</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Public administration refers to the totality of activities involved in implementing government policies, managing public resources, and delivering public services to citizens. It encompasses all non-judicial, non-legislative activities of government.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Features of Public Administration</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Concerned with implementing laws and policies made by the legislature and executive</li>\n  <li>Operates within a legal and constitutional framework</li>\n  <li>Not profit-driven — focused on public service, not commercial gain</li>\n  <li>Accountable to the public through elected representatives and the law</li>\n  <li>Hierarchical organisation — chain of command from ministers to junior officials</li>\n  <li>Permanent and professional — unlike political officeholders, public servants serve continuously</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Components of Public Administration</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Function</th><th>Example (Nigeria)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Civil Service</td><td>Permanent career bureaucracy implementing government policies</td><td>Federal Ministries (Finance, Education, Health)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Public Corporations</td><td>State-owned enterprises providing essential services</td><td>NEPA/PHCN, NNPC, NPA</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Regulatory Agencies</td><td>Regulating specific sectors in the public interest</td><td>CBN, NAFDAC, NCC, SEC</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Local Government Administration</td><td>Service delivery at the grassroots level</td><td>774 LGAs</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Principles of Good Public Administration</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Accountability:</strong> Officials are responsible for their actions and decisions</li>\n  <li><strong>Transparency:</strong> Open, accessible, and honest conduct of public affairs</li>\n  <li><strong>Rule of law:</strong> All actions within legal authority</li>\n  <li><strong>Efficiency:</strong> Achieving maximum results with minimum resources</li>\n  <li><strong>Equity:</strong> Fair treatment of all citizens without discrimination</li>\n  <li><strong>Responsiveness:</strong> Meeting the needs and expectations of citizens</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The key distinction between POLITICS and PUBLIC ADMINISTRATION: politicians (elected) MAKE policy decisions; public servants (appointed/permanent) IMPLEMENT those decisions. The 'politics-administration dichotomy' (Wilson, 1887) argues these should be strictly separated. In practice, senior civil servants also advise on policy.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Public administration = implementing government policy and managing public affairs. Features: policy implementation, legal framework, public service orientation, accountability, hierarchy, permanence. Components: civil service, public corporations, regulatory agencies, LGA administration. Principles: accountability, transparency, rule of law, efficiency, equity, responsiveness.\n</div>\n  ",
