    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">civil service</span> is the body of permanent, professional, non-political government employees who implement government policies, deliver public services, and advise ministers. It is the backbone of public administration — it carries on the work of government regardless of which party is in power.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Civil Service</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The civil service is the body of permanent, paid, professional employees of government (excluding the military, judiciary, and elected officials) who implement laws, deliver services, and provide expert advice to political office-holders.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Characteristics of the Civil Service</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Permanence:</strong> Civil servants serve beyond the tenure of individual governments — they provide continuity</li>\n  <li><strong>Political neutrality:</strong> Civil servants serve any government in power, regardless of party</li>\n  <li><strong>Anonymity:</strong> Civil servants work behind the scenes — ministers take public credit or blame</li>\n  <li><strong>Impartiality:</strong> Decisions based on merit, law, and professional judgment — not personal or political considerations</li>\n  <li><strong>Ministerial responsibility:</strong> Ministers are publicly accountable for their ministry's actions; civil servants advise but do not make political decisions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Structure of the Nigerian Civil Service</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Federal Civil Service:</strong> Federal Ministries, Departments, and Agencies (MDAs) in Abuja and across Nigeria</li>\n  <li><strong>State Civil Services:</strong> Each of the 36 states has its own civil service</li>\n  <li><strong>Hierarchy:</strong> Permanent Secretary → Director → Deputy Director → Assistant Director → Senior Officers → Officers → Executive/Clerical Officers</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Functions of the Civil Service</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Implementing government policies and programmes</li>\n  <li>Advising ministers on policy options and implications</li>\n  <li>Drafting bills and regulations for consideration by the legislature</li>\n  <li>Collecting revenue (taxes, fees, duties)</li>\n  <li>Delivering public services (education, health, security, infrastructure)</li>\n  <li>Maintaining government records and institutional memory</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Problems of the Nigerian Civil Service</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Corruption and misappropriation of public funds</li>\n  <li>Politicisation — appointments based on ethnicity/party rather than merit</li>\n  <li>Ghost workers — phantom employees collecting salaries</li>\n  <li>Inefficiency and bureaucratic bottlenecks</li>\n  <li>Low morale due to irregular salary payments</li>\n  <li>Lack of professionalism and training</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Three key characteristics of civil service: PERMANENCE (serves beyond government changes), POLITICAL NEUTRALITY (serves any party in power), and ANONYMITY (civil servants are not publicly visible; ministers take public responsibility). The Permanent Secretary is the most senior civil servant in a ministry.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Civil service = permanent, professional, politically neutral government employees. Key characteristics: permanence, neutrality, anonymity, impartiality. Functions: implement policy, advise ministers, draft laws, collect revenue, deliver services. Problems in Nigeria: corruption, ghost workers, politicisation, inefficiency. Structure: Permanent Secretary → Directors → Officers.\n</div>\n  ",
