    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Local government</span> is the third tier of government in Nigeria's federal system. It is the government closest to the people at the grassroots level, responsible for delivering basic services and promoting community development within defined local areas.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Local Government</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Local government is the lowest tier of government, established within defined local areas, with authority to manage local affairs, deliver services, and promote community welfare — deriving its powers from the constitution and state laws.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Functions of Local Government in Nigeria</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The Fourth Schedule of the 1999 Constitution specifies LG functions:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Primary education</td><td>Primary schools (shared with states)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Primary healthcare</td><td>Health centres and dispensaries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Markets and slaughterhouses</td><td>Establishing and regulating local markets</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Local roads and drains</td><td>Maintenance of streets and drainage systems</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sanitation and waste</td><td>Refuse collection and environmental cleanliness</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Birth and death registration</td><td>Civil registration of vital events</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Agricultural development</td><td>Assisting farmers, rural development</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Structure of Local Government in Nigeria</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>774 Local Government Areas (LGAs) across 36 states + FCT</li>\n  <li>Headed by an <strong>Executive Chairman</strong> (elected)</li>\n  <li><strong>Legislative Council:</strong> Elected councillors representing wards</li>\n  <li>Secretary of Local Government and departmental heads (administration)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Importance of Local Government</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Brings government closest to the people at grassroots level</li>\n  <li>Promotes democratic participation at community level</li>\n  <li>Facilitates grassroots development and rural transformation</li>\n  <li>Enables rapid response to local needs</li>\n  <li>Serves as training ground for democracy and political participation</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Problems of Local Government in Nigeria</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Interference by state governments in LGA affairs</li>\n  <li>Inadequate funding — heavy dependence on federal allocation</li>\n  <li>Corruption and mismanagement of funds by LG officials</li>\n  <li>Lack of qualified personnel</li>\n  <li>Political interference in LGA council elections</li>\n  <li>Poor service delivery to communities</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Nigeria has 774 LGAs (Local Government Areas). The head of LG is the EXECUTIVE CHAIRMAN (elected). LG functions are listed in the FOURTH SCHEDULE of the 1999 Constitution. LG is the THIRD TIER of government in Nigeria's federal system (Federal → State → Local).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Local government = third tier closest to people. Nigeria has 774 LGAs. Head: Executive Chairman. Functions (Fourth Schedule): primary education, healthcare, markets, roads, sanitation, birth/death registration. Importance: grassroots governance, democratic participation, local development. Problems: state interference, inadequate funding, corruption, poor delivery.\n</div>\n  ",
