    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  British colonial administration transformed Nigerian governance through conquest, amalgamation, and distinctive administrative systems. Two key policies — <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Direct Rule</span> (initially in the South) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Indirect Rule</span> (in the North) — shaped colonial governance and left lasting impacts on modern Nigeria.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Establishment of British Colonial Rule</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Britain established colonial control through military conquest, treaties, and commercial companies (Royal Niger Company)</li>\n  <li>1861: Lagos annexed as a Crown Colony</li>\n  <li>1900: Royal Niger Company's charter revoked; two Protectorates established (Northern and Southern Nigeria)</li>\n  <li>1906: Lagos Colony and Southern Nigeria Protectorate merged into Colony and Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</li>\n  <li>1914: Amalgamation of Northern and Southern Nigeria by Lord Frederick Lugard → creating the Colony and Protectorate of Nigeria</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Indirect Rule (Northern Nigeria and beyond)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Indirect Rule was the system developed by Lord Frederick Lugard in Northern Nigeria, where the British governed through existing traditional rulers (Emirs) rather than directly appointing British officials to govern at all levels.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>How it worked:</strong> British Residents advised (really directed) Emirs; Emirs continued to govern using native authority structures; Native Courts applied local law; Native Treasuries collected taxes</li>\n  <li><strong>Why it worked better in the North:</strong> The Sokoto Caliphate's centralised hierarchical system was ideal for Indirect Rule — Emirs had authority citizens respected and obeyed</li>\n  <li><strong>Merits:</strong> Cost-effective (fewer British officials needed); used existing legitimate authority; less social disruption; preserved local cultures</li>\n  <li><strong>Demerits:</strong> Undermined traditional rulers' independence; froze traditional institutions; slowed modernisation; used by British to maintain control</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Challenges of Indirect Rule in the South</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The decentralised Igbo system had no paramount chiefs — the British created 'Warrant Chiefs' (appointed local leaders) who lacked genuine authority</li>\n  <li>Warrant Chief Okugo's abuses and the Aba Women's Riot (1929) showed the failures of imposed indirect rule in Igbo areas</li>\n  <li>Yoruba states had existing Obas but were more familiar with British culture, reducing the need for rigid indirect rule</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Impact of Colonial Administration</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Amalgamation (1914) created the artificial boundaries of modern Nigeria — joining diverse peoples</li>\n  <li>Introduction of Western education, Christianity, and common law alongside Islamic institutions</li>\n  <li>Development of infrastructure (railways, roads, telegraph) for resource extraction</li>\n  <li>Economic exploitation — exported raw materials, controlled trade</li>\n  <li>Seeds of inequality between North and South (different educational and developmental trajectories)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Lord Lugard = amalgamated Nigeria in 1914 + developed Indirect Rule. Indirect Rule worked BEST in the NORTH (Sokoto Caliphate's hierarchy was perfect). It FAILED in the SOUTH (Igbo had no central chief → Warrant Chiefs created → Aba Women's Riot 1929). The 1914 Amalgamation is the origin of modern Nigeria.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Colonial administration: 1861 Lagos annexed; 1914 Amalgamation (Lord Lugard). Indirect Rule: governing through traditional rulers (Emirs). Worked well in North (centralised hierarchy), failed in South (no central chiefs → Warrant Chiefs → Aba Women's Riot 1929). Impact: modern Nigeria's borders, infrastructure, Western education, economic exploitation.\n</div>\n  ",
