    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Nationalism</span> in Nigeria refers to the political movement that sought to end British colonial rule and achieve self-governance and independence. Nigerian nationalism grew through political organisations, newspapers, and educated elites from the 1920s to independence in 1960.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Nationalism</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Nationalism is a political ideology that asserts that a people sharing a common identity, culture, or territory have the right to self-determination and self-governance. In colonial Africa, it manifested as the movement for independence from European colonial powers.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Causes/Factors that Promoted Nigerian Nationalism</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Western education:</strong> Educated Nigerians became aware of democratic ideals and questioned colonial rule</li>\n  <li><strong>The Second World War (1939–1945):</strong> Nigerian soldiers fought for Britain's freedom while denied their own; post-war idealism weakened colonialism globally</li>\n  <li><strong>The Atlantic Charter (1941):</strong> Roosevelt and Churchill's declaration of people's right to choose their own government</li>\n  <li><strong>Rise of political organisations:</strong> NCNC, AG, NPC mobilised Nigerians</li>\n  <li><strong>The press and newspapers:</strong> Herbert Macaulay's newspapers, Nnamdi Azikiwe's West African Pilot stirred nationalist consciousness</li>\n  <li><strong>Pan-Africanism:</strong> Marcus Garvey, W.E.B. Du Bois inspired African self-confidence and identity</li>\n  <li><strong>Economic exploitation:</strong> Resentment of colonial economic discrimination</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Key Nationalists and Their Contributions</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Nationalist</th><th>Contribution</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Herbert Macaulay (1864–1946)</td><td>'Father of Nigerian Nationalism' — founded NNDP (1923), nationalist newspaper, organised resistance; co-founded NCNC (1944)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nnamdi Azikiwe (Zik) (1904–1996)</td><td>Founded West African Pilot newspaper; president of NCNC; First Governor-General and First President of Nigeria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Obafemi Awolowo (1909–1987)</td><td>Founded Action Group (AG, 1951); Western Region Premier; authored nationalist books</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ahmadu Bello (1909–1966)</td><td>Sardauna of Sokoto; founded NPC (Northern People's Congress); Premier of Northern Region</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tafawa Balewa (1912–1966)</td><td>First Prime Minister of Nigeria; moderate Northern nationalist; NPC</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Major Political Organisations</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Nigerian National Democratic Party (NNDP, 1923):</strong> Founded by Herbert Macaulay — first Nigerian political party</li>\n  <li><strong>National Council of Nigeria and the Cameroons (NCNC, 1944):</strong> Founded by Herbert Macaulay and Nnamdi Azikiwe — pan-Nigerian nationalist movement</li>\n  <li><strong>Action Group (AG, 1951):</strong> Founded by Obafemi Awolowo — primarily Western Region</li>\n  <li><strong>Northern People's Congress (NPC, 1951):</strong> Founded by Ahmadu Bello — Northern Region</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Constitutional Developments Toward Independence</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Clifford Constitution (1922) — first elective principle</li>\n  <li>Richards Constitution (1946) — three regions created</li>\n  <li>Macpherson Constitution (1951) — wider participation, regional governments</li>\n  <li>Lyttleton Constitution (1954) — full federal structure, regional self-government</li>\n  <li>Independence Constitution (1960) — full independence on October 1, 1960</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Herbert Macaulay = 'Father of Nigerian Nationalism' (NNDP 1923 = first Nigerian political party). Nnamdi Azikiwe = first president of Nigeria (NCNC, West African Pilot newspaper). Nigeria gained independence on October 1, 1960, under Tafawa Balewa (PM) and Nnamdi Azikiwe (Governor-General).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nationalism = movement for self-governance/independence. Causes: Western education, WWII, Atlantic Charter, press, Pan-Africanism. Key figures: Herbert Macaulay (Father of Nationalism), Azikiwe (NCNC, first president), Awolowo (AG), Ahmadu Bello (NPC), Balewa (first PM). Constitutional milestones: Clifford (1922) → Richards (1946) → Macpherson (1951) → Lyttleton (1954) → Independence (1960).\n</div>\n  ",
